2026-09-23T07:12:29.0914377Z     The ``pytest`` framework makes it easy to write small tests, yet
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0914545Z     scales to support complex functional testing for applications and libraries.
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0914611Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0914695Z     An example of a simple test:
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0914756Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0914839Z     .. code-block:: python
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0914903Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0914991Z         # content of test_sample.py
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0915063Z         def inc(x):
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0915140Z             return x + 1
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0915204Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0915266Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0915342Z         def test_answer():
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0915427Z             assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0915490Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0915557Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0915633Z     To execute it::
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0915694Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0915773Z         $ pytest
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0915903Z         ============================= test session starts =============================
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0915981Z         collected 1 items
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0916043Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0916124Z         test_sample.py F
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0916192Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0916300Z         ================================== FAILURES ===================================
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0916513Z         _________________________________ test_answer _________________________________
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0916583Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0916664Z             def test_answer():
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0916741Z         >       assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0916821Z         E       assert 4 == 5
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0916901Z         E        +  where 4 = inc(3)
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0916964Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0917060Z         test_sample.py:5: AssertionError
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0917183Z         ========================== 1 failed in 0.04 seconds ===========================
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0917316Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0917387Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0917954Z     Thanks to ``pytest``'s detailed assertion introspection, you can simply use plain ``assert`` statements. See `getting-started <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html#our-first-test-run>`_ for more examples.
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0918020Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0918081Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0918151Z     Features
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0918224Z     --------
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0918377Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0918743Z     - Detailed info on failing `assert statements <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/assert.html>`_ (no need to remember ``self.assert*`` names)
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0918812Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0918891Z     - `Auto-discovery
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0919113Z       <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/goodpractices.html#python-test-discovery>`_
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0919200Z       of test modules and functions
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0919272Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0919471Z     - `Modular fixtures <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/fixtures.html>`_ for
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0919611Z       managing small or parametrized long-lived test resources
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0919677Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0919875Z     - Can run `unittest <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/unittest.html>`_ (or trial)
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0919953Z       test suites out of the box
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0920019Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0920107Z     - Python 3.10+ or PyPy3
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0920171Z     
2026-09-23T07:12:29.0920532Z     - Rich plugin architecture, with over 1300+ `external plugins <https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/plugin_list.html>`_ and thriving community
